(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-communityviolence"]={"plain":{"bills":{"communityviolence":{"slug":"communityviolence","title":"The Community Violence Reduction Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the \"Community Violence Reduction Act.\""},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":null,"body":"Congress finds as follows:\n\n1. In 2024, 44,447 people died by firearm in the United States, and more than half of those deaths were suicides. From 2009 through 2017, emergency departments recorded an average of about 85,700 visits each year for nonfatal firearm injury. Firearm injury is the leading cause of death for Americans between the ages of 1 and 19.\n2. Community violence intervention works where it is done with fidelity. Focused-deterrence partnerships between law enforcement and community organizations have produced repeated, substantial reductions in gun homicide across dozens of American cities, and hospital-based violence intervention programs substantially reduce violent reinjury among treated patients. The same evaluations show the effects decay when staffing, management, or funding lapse \u2014 so the failure mode this title must prevent is interruption, not ineffectiveness.\n3. At least eight States \u2014 California, Colorado, Connecticut, Illinois, Maryland, Michigan, New York, and Oregon \u2014 have authorized Medicaid payment for violence-prevention services. Where one payer has demonstrated that a service improves outcomes for patients, every payer should be able to furnish it.\n4. Between 1996 and 2019, the Federal Government conducted almost no research into firearm injury, while conducting extensive research into every comparable cause of death. In fiscal year 2020, the National Tracing Center of the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives received about 490,000 firearm trace requests from law enforcement agencies, yet appropriations riders bar nearly all analysis, publication, and scholarly use of what those traces show. A government that may not measure a cause of death cannot reduce it.\n5. Secure storage saves lives. Laws and practices that keep firearms from unsupervised access by children are supported by the strongest evidence rating in the firearm-policy literature, across youth suicide, homicide, and unintentional injury alike; and the barrier to secure storage is most often the price of the device.\n6. When a firearm is temporarily out of reach during a suicidal crisis, the crisis usually passes and the person usually lives. Institutions that have separated people in crisis from their firearms \u2014 including allied military forces that ended the practice of taking service weapons home on leave \u2014 have recorded large, immediate reductions in suicide. The tools in title IV of this Act are voluntary; their power comes from being available before the crisis, not from compelling anyone.\n7. Nothing in this Act restricts the acquisition or possession of any firearm by any person, and nothing in this Act creates, or authorizes the creation of, any registry of firearms or firearm owners.\n8. In a 2015 national probability survey of adults, 54 percent of firearm owners said that discussions of firearms by health care providers with patients are at least sometimes appropriate. The offers of titles IV and V reach the people at risk only if accepting them requires no disclosure and leaves no record that could follow the individual. The rules that the offers be made without inquiry into firearm ownership, and that they generate no individual-level record of acceptance, declination, or receipt, serve two interests: uptake of the offers by everyone to whom they are made, and the privacy of each individual's health and firearm information. Under existing law, a health care provider may disclose health information in response to a subpoena or discovery request not accompanied by a court order, on the assurances that regulation requires (45 CFR 164.512(e)(1)(ii))."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":null,"body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a)** \"Violence intervention professional\" means an individual who meets the standard stated in section 2-1(c) for an individual, through instruction in community violence intervention or hospital-based violence intervention, whether or not the program that gave the instruction appears on any list published under that section.\n\n**(b)** \"Qualified violence intervention program\" means a program described in section 1-2(a) that satisfies the conditions of section 1-2(b).\n\n**(c)** \"Secure storage device\" means a secure gun storage or safety device as defined in section 921(a)(34) of title 18, United States Code, that meets the performance standard stated in section 5-1.\n\n**(d)** \"Covered clinical encounter\" means a behavioral-health encounter, an emergency-department discharge following a suicide attempt or expression of suicidal ideation, or an intake assessment, in a health system described in section 4-2(a).\n\n**(e)** \"Voluntary do-not-transfer registration\" means a registration under the subsection added to section 40901 of title 34, United States Code, by section 4-1 of this Act.\n\n**(f)** \"State\" includes the District of Columbia, each territory of the United States, and, wherever a provision of this Act funds, protects, or recognizes, each federally recognized Indian Tribe; a provision of this Act binds a Tribal government only as to a function the Tribe has voluntarily assumed by compact or contract, and the omission of any further application to Tribal law is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. The Community Violence Intervention Fund.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 COMMUNITY VIOLENCE INTERVENTION","body":"**(a) Establishment.** There is established in the Treasury the Community Violence Intervention Fund. The Fund consists of amounts appropriated by subsection (b) and any amounts appropriated or transferred to the Fund under any other Act.\n\n**(b) Permanent appropriation.** There is appropriated to the Fund, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, for each fiscal year, the annual amount set in the SCHEDULE, to remain available until expended. Amounts in the Fund are available without further appropriation and without fiscal year limitation, and shall be obligated for the purposes of this title in each fiscal year. No appropriation or transfer to the Fund under any other Act reduces the amount appropriated by this subsection.\n\n**(c) Continuity.** The availability of amounts in the Fund, and every function this title requires, continue without interruption during any lapse in other appropriations. Section 9-4(b) governs the officers and employees who perform those functions during a lapse.\n\n**(d) Use.** Amounts in the Fund shall be used only \u2014\n  1. for awards to qualified violence intervention programs under section 1-3;\n  2. for the independent evaluations required by section 1-4, for which the percentage of the Fund set in the SCHEDULE is reserved; and\n  3. for administration, for which not more than the percentage of the Fund set in the SCHEDULE may be used.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 COMMUNITY VIOLENCE INTERVENTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Qualified violence intervention programs.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 COMMUNITY VIOLENCE INTERVENTION","body":"**(a) Program classes.** A program is described in this subsection if it is \u2014\n  1. a focused-deterrence or group-violence-intervention partnership among law enforcement agencies, community organizations, and social-service providers, directed at the individuals and groups at highest identified risk of committing or suffering gun violence;\n  2. a hospital-based or hospital-linked violence intervention program furnishing services to patients treated for violent injury; or\n  3. a street-outreach or violence-interruption program employing violence intervention professionals.\n\n**(b) Conditions of qualification.** A program described in subsection (a) is a qualified violence intervention program if, and for so long as, each of the following is true:\n  1. The program operates under a written model fidelity plan identifying the evaluated model it implements, the staffing and management structure that model requires, and the outcome measures it will report.\n  2. The program reports the outcome measures identified in its fidelity plan to the Attorney General semiannually. A report that omits an outcome measure identified in the program's fidelity plan, or reports a measure without the underlying counts, does not satisfy this paragraph. Each report also states the area in which the program served individuals during the reporting period and the number of individuals it served; a report does not fail this paragraph by reason of either statement.\n  3. The program participates in any evaluation under section 1-4 for which it is selected. A program does not fail this paragraph by declining any part of an evaluation design that would deny, defer, or reduce services to an individual eligible for them while the program has capacity to serve that individual.\n\n**(c) Consequence.** A program that has failed to satisfy subsection (b) for two consecutive reporting periods is not a qualified violence intervention program until it has satisfied subsection (b) for one full reporting period. No finding, determination, or exercise of discretion by any officer is required for, or may delay, the operation of this subsection.\n\n**(d) Collection of information.** The Attorney General shall submit any collection of information necessary to carry out subsection (b)(2) under section 3507 of title 44, United States Code, not later than 180 days after enactment. On and after the earlier of the date a control number is assigned to that collection or one year after enactment, section 3512 of that title does not bar the operation of subsection (c). The officer who publishes a design under section 1-4(a-1) shall submit any collection of information necessary to the evaluation under section 3507 of that title not later than 180 days after the publication. On and after the earlier of the date a control number is assigned to that collection or one year after the publication, section 3507(a) of that title does not bar the collection, and section 3512 of that title does not bar the operation of subsection (b)(3) or (c) as to the evaluation; every other requirement of chapter 35 of that title continues to apply."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Awards.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 COMMUNITY VIOLENCE INTERVENTION","body":"**(a) Awards.** The Attorney General shall make awards from the Fund to qualified violence intervention programs not later than October 31 of each fiscal year. Awards shall fund evaluated models, not named vendors, and no award may be conditioned on the identity of the organization operating a model rather than on qualification under section 1-2.\n\n**(a-1) Continuation.** Each qualified violence intervention program that held an award in the preceding fiscal year shall receive awards for the fiscal year totaling not less than its continuation amount. A program's continuation amount is the amount of its award for the preceding fiscal year adjusted by the percentage change from the preceding fiscal year in the amount appropriated by section 1-1(b); except that, where the continuation amounts of all such programs qualified on October 1 of the fiscal year together exceed the award base reduced by the percentage set in the SCHEDULE for subsection (c), each continuation amount for the fiscal year is reduced by the percentage that makes those continuation amounts together equal the award base so reduced.\n\n**(a-2) Increases and the reserve follow results.** No award beyond a program's continuation amount, and no amount reserved by subsection (c), may be made to a program if the most recent evaluation, completed under section 1-4 on a design meeting section 1-4(a-1), of the model its fidelity plan identifies under section 1-2(b)(1), is null or unfavorable. An evaluation is null or unfavorable if the report of the entity that conducted it does not find an effect favorable to the model on the primary outcome measure its design identifies, at the level of statistical confidence its design states. Such an evaluation does not affect qualification under section 1-2, the continuation amount, or the provision of services to any individual.\n\n**(a-3) Award base, first charge, counting, and identity.** For purposes of this section \u2014\n  1. A fiscal year's award base is the sum of the amount appropriated by section 1-1(b) for the fiscal year and the amounts appropriated or transferred to the Fund under any other Act in the preceding fiscal year, each as reduced by any sequestration, less the percentages of that sum set in the SCHEDULE for section 1-1(d)(2) and (3).\n  2. Continuation amounts, as reduced under subsection (a-1), are a first charge on the award base; no award may be made from the award base to the extent it would leave unobligated less than the continuation amounts not yet received, except an award under subsection (c) to the extent that it and the other awards under that subsection for the fiscal year do not exceed the percentage of the award base set in the SCHEDULE for that subsection.\n  3. An award is made to, and received by, a program when it is obligated. A program's award for a fiscal year is the total of its awards counted in that year. Each amount obligated under an award is counted in each fiscal year in proportion to the months of the period that amount is obligated to fund that fall in that year, whatever the award's period of performance. An allocation under subsection (b) is counted in full in the fiscal year for which it is made, from November 30 of that year, whether or not it has been obligated or disbursed.\n  4. A program remains the same program through any amendment of its fidelity plan or change in the entity operating it, so long as it serves any part of the area it served in the preceding fiscal year. Programs that combine hold the sum of their continuation amounts, and a program that divides apportions its continuation amount among the resulting programs in proportion to the individuals each served in the most recent reporting period.\n  5. The entity that held a program's award for the preceding fiscal year, or an entity that has succeeded it in operating the program, holds the program's continuation amount, the right to payment under each award counted toward it, any allocation under subsection (b), and any action under subsection (b-1). A successor holds them by operation of this paragraph, and section 3727 of title 31, United States Code, does not apply to the succession. A payment made to the entity the United States last paid, before the United States receives written notice of a succession, discharges the United States to the extent of the payment; and no notice diverts payment from an entity that continues to operate the program.\n\n**(b) Allocation by operation of law.** If, on November 30 of any fiscal year, a program that is a qualified violence intervention program on that date and that held an award in the preceding fiscal year has not received awards for that fiscal year totaling at least its continuation amount, the program is allocated, by operation of this subsection and without further act by any person, the amount by which its continuation amount exceeds those awards, and the Secretary of the Treasury shall disburse each such allocation. An allocation under this subsection is an award under this section, payable from the Fund, and an allocation exceeding the amounts then available is disbursed as amounts become available. The Attorney General shall certify to the Secretary of the Treasury, under section 3325 of title 31, United States Code, a voucher for each such allocation, or for the part of it payable from the amounts then available, not later than December 15 of the fiscal year, and a voucher for each later part not later than 15 days after amounts become available to pay it. Where the amounts available do not pay in full every allocation then unpaid, they are applied to those allocations ratably, in proportion to the unpaid amount of each. An allocation carries the terms and conditions of the program's most recent award under this section or, where it has none, of awards under subsection (a) for the fiscal year, for a period of performance of the fiscal year for which it is made; those terms govern its use and reporting only, are not the source of the program's right to it, and permit its termination or withholding on no ground other than one subsection (b-1) states. This subsection supplies the allocation; it relieves no officer of the duty stated in subsection (a) or (a-1).\n\n**(b-1) Protection and enforcement.** An award counted toward a program's continuation amount, and any allocation under subsection (b), may not be terminated, suspended, or reduced, and payment under it may not be withheld, except for the program's noncompliance with the terms of the award or its ceasing to be a qualified violence intervention program; a termination, suspension, reduction, or withholding on any other ground is without effect. A program's right to its continuation amount, to payment under each award counted toward it, and to any allocation arises under this section and not under any agreement. The duties of the Attorney General under subsections (a-1) and (b), of the Secretary of the Treasury under subsection (b), and of each officer to make payment under an award counted toward a program's continuation amount, are ministerial and are owed to the program, which may bring a civil action in the district court of the United States for the district in which it operates, or for the District of Columbia, to enforce them. The district courts have jurisdiction of the action, the United States consents to it, the relief it seeks is specific relief and not money damages, and section 1491 of title 28, United States Code, does not apply to it. If the preceding sentence is held invalid in any respect, subsection (b), and this subsection as to payment under an award counted toward a program's continuation amount, are each a provision of law mandating the payment of money for purposes of section 1491(a)(1) of that title. Relief on a claim to enforce a duty this subsection states is an order directing the award, certification, payment, or disbursement of the amount owed; a declaratory judgment; any further order necessary to carry out those orders; and costs. Where the court finds that such a duty has not been performed as this section requires, it shall order its performance not later than the earliest date on which it finds the duty can be performed, and neither the defendant's other priorities, nor the resources available to the defendant other than the amounts in the Fund available to make the payment, nor any other equitable consideration is a ground for withholding that order. For purposes of section 2412 of title 28, United States Code, no provision of this section shall be treated as otherwise specifically providing for, or as expressly prohibiting, an award of costs, fees, or other expenses under that section; and the relief this subsection states supports no inference as to the relief on a claim to enforce a duty that any other provision of this Act makes enforceable.\n\n**(c) New programs.** Not less than the percentage of annual awards set in the SCHEDULE shall be reserved for programs not funded in the preceding fiscal year that operate in a community whose rate of firearm homicide exceeds the national rate. For purposes of this subsection, a community is the county, or the place delineated by the Bureau of the Census, in which the program operates, as the applicant elects; and each rate is the average annual rate of firearm homicide among residents over the number of years set in the SCHEDULE ending with the most recent year for which the National Vital Statistics System of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention has final mortality data, computed not later than August 1 before each fiscal year by the Director of the National Center for Health Statistics from that System's final mortality data and the population estimates of the Bureau of the Census for the same years. The Director shall state to any applicant, on request, whether the rate for each county and each place in which it operates exceeds the national rate, and shall state no count of deaths in doing so."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Evaluation and publication.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 COMMUNITY VIOLENCE INTERVENTION","body":"**(a) Independent evaluation.** The Attorney General shall provide, by contract with entities independent of every funded program and of every person that developed, licenses, or trains programs in the model evaluated, and under common ownership or control with none of them, for evaluation of funded programs, using designs capable of supporting causal inference wherever assignment permits. No evaluation design may deny, defer, or reduce services to an individual eligible for a funded program's services while the program has capacity to serve that individual. For purposes of this subsection and section 1-2(b)(3), a program has capacity to serve an individual while it is serving fewer individuals than its staff employed on the date of the individual's assignment can serve at the caseload per staff member that its fidelity plan identified, or if it identified none that the evaluated model requires, on the date the program was selected for the evaluation; and an individual is eligible if the individual meets the criteria for the program's services in effect on that date. Each design shall meet subsection (a-1). Where an evaluation does not include every funded program implementing the evaluated model, the programs it includes are selected by lot from among those programs. Each model the fidelity plan of a funded program identifies shall be evaluated at least once in each period of the number of years set in the SCHEDULE, to the extent the amount reserved by section 1-1(d)(2) permits a design meeting subsection (a-1), in an order set by a lot the Attorney General draws among the models not yet evaluated in the period. The Attorney General shall publish the method of each lot this subsection requires before the lot is drawn, naming a published source of random values that the Attorney General cannot know or influence when the method is published, and shall publish the result of each lot when it is drawn and before any evaluation whose order or programs it sets begins.\n\n**(a-1) Design standard.** Before data collection begins, each design shall state, and the Attorney General shall publish, its primary outcome measure, which is a measure of firearm injury, violent injury or reinjury, or violent victimization or offending among the individuals or in the areas the evaluated programs serve and, where the fidelity plans of those programs identify such a measure, is one of those measures; a level of statistical confidence, two-sided, of not more than 95 percent; and a power of not less than 80 percent, at that level, to detect a reduction in the primary outcome measure of the percentage set in the SCHEDULE. A design that does not do each of these things does not meet this subsection. An evaluation is completed on the date the entity that conducted it delivers to the Attorney General the report it designates as final.\n\n**(b) Publication.** The Attorney General shall publish, not later than April 1 of each year, a report stating, for each funded program: the model implemented; the amounts awarded and disbursed; each outcome measure reported under section 1-2(b)(2) with its underlying counts; and the results of every evaluation completed in the preceding year, including null and unfavorable results. A publication that omits any funded program, any reported outcome measure, or any completed evaluation does not satisfy this subsection. The duty stated in this subsection is ministerial and enforceable by mandamus by any person.\n\n**(c) Confidentiality.** Section 10231(a) of title 34, United States Code, applies to research or statistical information identifiable to a private person that is furnished for an evaluation under this section as it applies to information furnished under the chapter in which that section appears. As to that information only, each reference in section 10231(a) to \"this chapter\" is a reference to this section, and its reference to the Office of Justice Programs is a reference to the Attorney General; and the restriction section 10231(a) places on officers, employees, and recipients of assistance binds as well each entity conducting an evaluation under this section, each of its contractors and subcontractors at any tier, and each funded program, as to that information."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Medicaid coverage of violence prevention services.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PAYMENT FOR VIOLENCE PREVENTION SERVICES","body":"**(a) Benefit.** Section 1905(a)(13) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1396d(a)(13)) is amended by inserting after \"rehabilitative services,\" the following: \"and violence prevention services (as defined in subsection (ll)),\". As amended, the paragraph will read:\n\n> (13) other diagnostic, screening, preventive, and rehabilitative services, **and violence prevention services (as defined in subsection (ll)),** including\u2014 \u2026\n\n**(b) Definition; Federal share; cost sharing.** Section 1905 of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1396d) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(ll) Violence prevention services.** (1) The term \"violence prevention services\" means evidence-informed services furnished to an individual who has been treated for a violent injury, or who is at elevated risk of violent injury or violent reinjury, by a violence intervention professional (as defined in section 0-3(a) of the Community Violence Reduction Act), including risk assessment, safety planning, mentorship, service navigation, and follow-up engagement.\n> (2) Notwithstanding subsection (b), the Federal medical assistance percentage for amounts expended for violence prevention services furnished to an individual who has been treated for a violent injury, or for whom a clinical determination of elevated risk of violent injury or violent reinjury, made by a physician or other licensed practitioner of the healing arts within the scope of practice under State law, is documented in the individual's medical record before the services are furnished, is 90 percent or, if greater, the percentage that would apply to those amounts without regard to this paragraph. The Federal medical assistance percentage for amounts expended for any other violence prevention services is the percentage that would apply to those amounts without regard to this paragraph.\n> (3) Notwithstanding sections 1916 and 1916A, no deduction, cost sharing, or similar charge may be imposed with respect to violence prevention services.\n> (4) This subsection does not limit medical assistance under subsection (a)(13) for preventive or rehabilitative services furnished by a practitioner meeting qualifications the State sets, including services that would be violence prevention services if furnished by a violence intervention professional.\n\n**(c) Professional standard.** An individual meets the standard of this subsection if the individual has completed a training program's instruction in violence intervention practice, trauma-informed care, safety planning, and confidentiality, and has passed an assessment of competency in each of those subjects given by a body independent of that training program. A training program meets the standard of this subsection if it provides that instruction and issues a certification only to an individual who meets the standard of this subsection. A body is independent of a training program for purposes of this subsection only if it neither provides the program's instruction nor is under common ownership or control with any person that does, and no compensation it receives from or through the program, or from or through any person under common ownership or control with the program, depends on whether an individual passes. The Secretary of Health and Human Services shall publish a list of programs meeting the standard; the list is declaratory, and an individual who meets the standard of this subsection qualifies whether or not the program that gave the instruction appears on the list.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 PAYMENT FOR VIOLENCE PREVENTION SERVICES","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Medicare coverage of violence prevention services.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PAYMENT FOR VIOLENCE PREVENTION SERVICES","body":"**(a) Benefit.** Section 1861(s)(2) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1395x(s)(2)) is amended by adding at the end the following new subparagraph:\n\n> (LL) violence prevention services (as defined in subsection (ooo));\n\n**(b) Definition.** Section 1861 of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1395x) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(ooo) Violence prevention services.** The term \"violence prevention services\" has the meaning given that term in section 1905(ll)(1), furnished by a violence intervention professional under a plan of care established following treatment for a violent injury or a clinical determination of elevated risk of violent injury.\n\n**(c) Exclusion carve-out.** Section 1862(a)(1)(A) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1395y(a)(1)(A)) is amended by inserting \", or violence prevention services (as defined in section 1861(ooo))\" after \"multi-cancer early detection screening tests (as defined in section 1861(nnn))\". As amended, the subparagraph will provide that the reasonable-and-necessary exclusion applies \"except for items and services described in a succeeding subparagraph, additional preventive services (as described in section 1861(ddd)(1)), or multi-cancer early detection screening tests (as defined in section 1861(nnn))**, or violence prevention services (as defined in section 1861(ooo))**\".\n\n**(d) Conforming amendments.** Section 1833(a)(1) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1395l(a)(1)) is amended to provide payment for violence prevention services in an amount equal to 80 percent of the payment basis determined under the fee schedule applicable under section 1848, and the Secretary shall establish the applicable coding not later than the effective date stated in section 9-3. Pending establishment of coding, a claim for violence prevention services may be submitted under the applicable unlisted-service code and shall be paid at the rate for the most closely comparable coded service; the absence of a code relieves no payment obligation."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Firearm injury research.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RESEARCH AND DATA RESTORATION","body":"**(a) Program.** The Director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and the Director of the National Institutes of Health shall each conduct and support research into the causes, prevention, and reduction of firearm injury and death, through the peer-review processes applicable to other research those agencies support.\n\n**(b) Permanent appropriation.** There is appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, for each fiscal year, the annual amount set in the SCHEDULE, half to each agency, to remain available until expended, and available without further appropriation.\n\n**(c) Research is not advocacy.** Research conducted or supported under this section, and the publication of its results whatever they show, shall not be treated as advocacy or promotion for purposes of any restriction on the use of appropriated funds.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 RESEARCH AND DATA RESTORATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. National Violent Death Reporting System.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RESEARCH AND DATA RESTORATION","body":"The Director of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention shall operate the National Violent Death Reporting System in every State, territory, and participating Tribal jurisdiction. There is appropriated for that purpose, for each fiscal year, the annual amount set in the SCHEDULE, on the terms stated in section 3-1(b)."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Repeal of data restrictions.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RESEARCH AND DATA RESTORATION","body":"**(a) Trace-data secrecy.** The matter under the heading \"Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives \u2014 Salaries and Expenses\" in title II of division B of Public Law 112-55 (18 U.S.C. 923 note; 125 Stat. 609-610) is amended by striking the sixth proviso; and each predecessor proviso restricting disclosure of Firearms Trace System data in Public Law 111-117 (123 Stat. 3128-3129), Public Law 111-8 (123 Stat. 574-576), Public Law 110-161 (121 Stat. 1903-1904), Public Law 109-108 (119 Stat. 2295-2296), and Public Law 108-447 (118 Stat. 2859-2860), each set out as a note under section 923 of title 18, United States Code, is amended by striking its words of futurity (\"and thereafter\" or \"with respect to any fiscal year,\" as the case may be) so that it has no application after the fiscal year of its enactment.\n\n**(b) Freedom of Information Act restoration.** Section 644 of division J of Public Law 108-7 (5 U.S.C. 552 note; 117 Stat. 473) is repealed.\n\n**(c) Physical inventory.** The fifth proviso under the heading \"Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives \u2014 Salaries and Expenses\" in title II of division B of Public Law 113-6 (18 U.S.C. 923 note; 127 Stat. 248) is amended by striking \"and any fiscal year thereafter\".\n\n**(d) Records consolidation proviso.** The first proviso under the heading \"Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives \u2014 Salaries and Expenses\" in title II of division B of Public Law 112-55 (18 U.S.C. 923 note; 125 Stat. 609) is amended by striking \"herein or hereafter\" and inserting \"herein\".\n\n**(e) What this section does not do.** This section does not amend section 511 of Public Law 112-55 (18 U.S.C. 922 note; 125 Stat. 632), and nothing in this Act extends the retention of any identifying information that section requires to be destroyed. The omission is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Annual trace publication.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RESEARCH AND DATA RESTORATION","body":"**(a) Duty.** The Attorney General shall publish, not later than March 31 of each year, a statistical report of firearm trace data for the preceding calendar year stating, at the national level and for each State: the number of traces; time-to-recovery distributions; the categories of crime associated with traced firearms; the States of first retail sale ranked by trace frequency; the licensed dealers ranked by the frequency with which traced firearms were traced to the dealer as the last licensee known to have held them, whatever the dealer's records show as to its disposition, including a retail sale, a loss, a theft, or no disposition, identifying each dealer that is among the number of most-traced dealers set in the SCHEDULE, together with each dealer whose frequency equals that of the last dealer so identified; the licensed dealers of first retail sale ranked by the number of traced firearms recovered within the period after first retail sale set in the SCHEDULE, identifying separately each dealer ranked, in that ranking, within the number of dealers identified as most-traced under this subsection, together with each dealer whose number equals that of the last dealer so identified in that ranking; and multiple-sale and trafficking-indicator aggregates. Where section 6-5 of the Gun Trafficking Act requires a ranking of most-traced dealers to be published not later than January 31 of the year in which the report is due, the dealers identified as most-traced under this subsection are those that ranking identifies, whether or not it is published, and the report restates that identification. In a year in which no such ranking is required, the identification under this subsection is made as if the preceding sentence were not enacted; and to the extent that ranking or its publication is, on the date the report is due, held invalid or enjoined, the identification is made, to that extent, as if the preceding sentence were not enacted. For each ranking of licensed dealers this subsection requires, a firearm is traced in the calendar year in which the records of the National Tracing Center first show its trace identifying the dealer as the last licensee known to have held it or, for the ranking of dealers of first retail sale, as that dealer, or, where those records do not show when they first showed that identification, in the calendar year in which they show the trace completed; a licensee to which a dealer's records show a disposition of a firearm is the last licensee known to have held it only if that licensee's records, or its response to the trace, show its acquisition of it; and each ranking of licensed dealers this subsection requires is computed from the trace records the National Tracing Center holds at the close of January 15 of the year in which the report is due and is not revised on later records. However a trace identified the dealer as the last licensee known to have held a firearm, including through the records of the licensees in the chain of distribution or through information the dealer submitted under section 923(g)(3)(A), (5)(A), or (6) of title 18, United States Code, or section 6-6 of the Gun Trafficking Act, the firearm is traced to the dealer under this subsection; the naming of any means limits no other, and no means makes a licensee the last licensee known to have held a firearm where this subsection provides that it is not. The Attorney General shall preserve, as they stood at that close, the trace records from which each ranking of licensed dealers this subsection requires is to be computed or from which the Attorney General computes such a ranking, limited to the trace number; the license number of each licensed dealer for which the trace is counted in a ranking, and the date the records first show the identification for which it is counted; the dates of first retail sale and of recovery of the firearm; the State for which the trace is counted; for each licensed dealer for which the trace is counted in the ranking of licensed dealers by the frequency with which traced firearms were traced to the dealer, whether the records show that the trace identified the dealer only through information the dealer submitted under section 923(g)(5)(A) of title 18, United States Code, or section 6-6 of the Gun Trafficking Act, or do not show by what means the trace identified the dealer; and any other element of the record on which this subsection makes the counting of the trace depend, but in no case the name or other identifying information of any individual purchaser or possessor, which limit does not reach the license number this sentence requires; until the later of three years after the report is due and the final disposition of any action concerning it. The duties stated in this subsection, to publish the report and to preserve the records this subsection requires to be preserved, are ministerial, are owed to each person entitled to receive the published information, and are enforceable by mandamus by any person, and a report that omits any element enumerated in this subsection, or any content subsection (b) states for the identification of a licensee, does not satisfy it. The duty to preserve the records this subsection requires to be preserved is also owed to, and enforceable by mandamus by, each person, whether or not it is or was a licensee, that those records place among the dealers that a ranking required by this subsection is to identify, or that is identified by a ranking computed from them, by the ranking whose identification the report is required to restate, or by the report, and each party to an action concerning the report or the ranking whose identification the report is required to restate, including an action to compel the publication of the report or of that ranking, whether or not its enforcement by any person is held invalid. Relief on a claim to enforce a duty that this subsection makes enforceable by mandamus is an order directing the duty's performance; a declaratory judgment; any further order necessary to carry out those orders, other than an order that restrains or delays a publication; and costs. Where the court finds that such a duty has not been performed as this subsection requires, it shall order the duty's performance not later than the earliest date on which it finds the duty can be performed, and neither the defendant's other priorities, nor the resources available to the defendant, nor any other equitable consideration is a ground for withholding that order. An order directing a publication directs publication, and not a disclosure to the plaintiff alone; each such duty is owed to the plaintiff whole, and its performance in full is the complete relief to which the plaintiff is entitled. For purposes of section 2412 of title 28, United States Code, no provision of this section shall be treated as otherwise specifically providing for, or as expressly prohibiting, an award of costs, fees, or other expenses under that section; and the relief this subsection states supports no inference as to the relief on a claim to enforce a duty that any other provision of this Act makes enforceable. A report does not fail this subsection by omitting what a judgment or order then in effect forbids it to state, if it states that an identification is omitted under an order and names the order.\n\n**(b) Privacy floor.** A report under this section shall contain no name or identifying information of any individual purchaser or possessor, and no cell aggregating fewer than the number of records set in the SCHEDULE. This subsection does not reach the identification of a licensee that subsection (a) requires, which states the licensee's name; its license number; the number that places it in the ranking; for a licensee identified as most-traced under subsection (a), of the number that places it among the most-traced dealers, the number whose trace identified the licensee only through information the licensee submitted under section 923(g)(5)(A) of title 18, United States Code, or section 6-6 of the Gun Trafficking Act, and the number for which the records do not show by what means the trace identified the licensee; and the address of its business premises or, for a licensee whose premises is its private residence, its county.\n\n**(c) Relation to other law.** Section 552a(b) of title 5, United States Code, does not bar any disclosure a report under this section requires. Where a report under this section carries the statement section 514 of division B of Public Law 113-6 (127 Stat. 271; 18 U.S.C. 923 note) requires, that statement qualifies no duty or identification this section states, and no consequence any Act attaches to an identification in the report."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Records modernization without a registry.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 RESEARCH AND DATA RESTORATION","body":"**(a) Digitization.** The Attorney General shall convert the out-of-business licensee records held by the National Tracing Center to searchable electronic form and shall complete the conversion not later than three years after enactment. The duty stated in this subsection is ministerial and enforceable by mandamus.\n\n**(b) Permitted queries \u2014 a closed list.** The records converted under subsection (a), and every index of them, may be queried only \u2014\n  1. to complete a trace requested by a law enforcement agency in connection with a bona fide criminal investigation, by a query keyed to characteristics of a specific firearm, which may include a partial serial number; no query under this paragraph may be keyed to any characteristic of a purchaser, possessor, or transferee of a firearm; or\n  2. to compile the aggregate statistics section 3-4 requires.\nNo other query, use, extraction, or compilation is permitted, regardless of form, title, characterization, or the authority invoked, and no authority under this or any other Act may be construed to permit one.\n\n**(c) No registry.** No officer, employee, or agency of the United States may establish, maintain, or operate any registry of firearms or of firearm owners. Nothing in this Act, and no system built under it, creates such a registry or authorizes one; a query log maintained for audit under subsection (d) is not a registry and may not be aggregated into one.\n\n**(d) Audit.** Every query under subsection (b) shall be logged, and the Inspector General of the Department of Justice shall audit the log annually and publish the count of queries by predicate, together with every instance of a query outside subsection (b), listing separately each query made to complete a trace that was not keyed as subsection (b)(1) requires."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Voluntary do-not-transfer registration.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 SUICIDE PREVENTION AND MEANS SAFETY","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 40901 of title 34, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following new subsection:\n\n> **(m) Voluntary do-not-transfer registration.** (1) An individual may, on proof of identity, register with the national instant criminal background check system a request that the system advise licensees that a transfer of a firearm to that individual may not proceed. The Attorney General shall provide a form by which an individual may register online, giving proof of identity in the same submission, and that enters the registration in the system when it is submitted, without further act by any person; the system may accept a registration in any other form, and a registration may also be made by certified mail to the Federal Bureau of Investigation. A registration made online is effective when it is submitted with the proof of identity the form requires. Any other registration is effective when the system records it or, if the system has not recorded it sooner, on the seventh day after it is made or mailed; except that a registration made by mail is effective no sooner than the day on which the registrant completes an in-person identity check, which the Attorney General shall provide through a Federal agency. Each registration is effective by operation of this subsection. A record made by any person in verifying the proof of identity given for a registration or removal is information described in paragraph (5) and shall be destroyed on verification, and the person who makes it may disclose to no person the purpose of the verification. The duties to provide the online form and the in-person identity check are ministerial, are owed to each individual who may register, and are enforceable by mandamus by any such individual, who may proceed under a pseudonym with the individual's identity kept under seal.\n> (2) While a registration is effective, the Attorney General shall cause the system to respond to a background check on the registrant as it responds when a transfer would violate section 922 of title 18 \u2014 except that the response shall carry no code, flag, or designation distinguishable by the licensee from that response.\n> (3) A registrant may request removal at any time; removal is effective on the twenty-first day after the request.\n> (4) Registration is free. No fee may be charged for registration or removal.\n> (5) Information that an individual registered, was offered registration, declined registration, or requested removal \u2014 (A) may be used only to operate this subsection; (B) may not be recorded in any other system of records, disclosed to any person, or used in any determination of any right, benefit, employment, clearance, custody, or privilege; (C) is inadmissible, and may not be the basis of testimony, in any proceeding, civil, criminal, or administrative, other than a prosecution for a fraudulent registration of another person; and (D) shall be destroyed within 24 hours after a removal becomes effective. The uses stated in this paragraph are the only uses; the omission of any other use is a determination of Congress, and no common-law rule supplies one.\n> (6) No liability of any kind arises from registering, declining to register, or removing a registration, and no duty to register may be imposed by any person as a condition of anything.\n> (7) A registration under this subsection is not a registration of firearms, firearm owners, or firearm transactions or dispositions within the meaning of subsection (i)(2); it exists solely at the registrant's own election, may not be aggregated with any other record, and is subject to the exclusive uses and destruction rule of paragraph (5). Subsection (i) is otherwise unaffected, and the omission of any broader exception to subsection (i) is a determination of Congress.\n> (8) For purposes of sections 925B and 925C of title 18, and of any other provision of law requiring or permitting a report, notice, or referral of a denied transfer to any person other than the licensee that contacted the system, a response under paragraph (2) is not a notice that the receipt of a firearm would violate subsection (g) or (n) of section 922 of title 18 or State, local, or Tribal law and is not a denial, and no such report, notice, or referral may be made of it. Section 922(t)(2)(A) and (B) of title 18 do not apply to a background check on a registrant while a registration is effective. Every record of such a check, other than its transaction number and the date the number was assigned, shall be destroyed not later than 24 hours after the response, and until then is information described in paragraph (5).\n\n**(b) Offer is not inquiry.** An offer of registration under section 4-2 is not an inquiry into firearm ownership, and no response to it may be documented at the individual level.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 SUICIDE PREVENTION AND MEANS SAFETY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Offers at clinical encounters.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 SUICIDE PREVENTION AND MEANS SAFETY","body":"**(a) Covered systems.** This section applies to the Veterans Health Administration, the Military Health System, the Indian Health Service and any Tribal health program that elects by compact or contract to participate, and each Federally qualified health center (as defined in section 1861(aa)(4) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1395x(aa)(4))). Each duty and limit this section places on a covered system that is not an agency of the United States, and the limit section 4-1(b) states as it applies to such a covered system, binds the covered system by force of this section and is also, from enactment, a condition of its receipt of devices under section 5-2. If either basis is held invalid as to any covered system, duty, limit, or application, the other basis stands as to it; the two bases are not one scheme, and the invalidity of one supports no inference about the other.\n\n**(b) Duty.** Each covered system shall adopt, within one year of enactment, a standard protocol under which, at every covered clinical encounter, the individual is offered \u2014 1. lethal-means counseling; 2. enrollment in the voluntary do-not-transfer registration; and 3. a secure storage device at no charge, of each class section 5-2(b) permits to be furnished at that encounter, from stock funded under section 5-2.\n\n**(b-1) During a lapse in appropriations.** During any lapse in appropriations, each covered system shall make the offers subsection (b) requires at every covered clinical encounter it conducts. No officer or employee of the United States may direct the omission of an offer, or the withdrawal of device stock furnished under section 5-2, by reason of the lapse.\n\n**(c) One way.** The offer shall be made without inquiry into whether the individual owns a firearm. No individual-level record of acceptance or declination may be created; a covered system shall report annually only the aggregate count of covered clinical encounters it conducted, the aggregate count of encounters at which offers were made, and the aggregate count of devices furnished. The report shall state, in addition, for each State in which the covered system operates and for each class of device section 5-2(b) describes: the aggregate count of requests for a device of that class, among all requests the covered system receives at a visit or other contact at which section 5-2(b) permits a device of that class to be furnished, at which no device of that class was furnished at the visit or other contact at which it was requested, and the cause stated by category \u2014 want of stock, want of an eligible device, or another cause; the aggregate count of other requests for a device of that class, which is stated separately and is not a count of requests for purposes of subsection (c-1); the aggregate count of notices issued under subsection (c-2); and the aggregate count of devices not held by the date subsection (c-2)(2) requires. A request unfilled because no device of the requested class meeting the standard of section 5-1 was on hand is reported as want of stock or want of an eligible device, and never as another cause. A request on which a notice was issued under subsection (c-2), and for which no device was held by the date subsection (c-2)(2) requires, is reported as unfilled for want of stock, in place of any other cause, and is counted once. Each covered system shall submit the report to the Secretary of Health and Human Services, and publish it on a publicly accessible website, not later than March 31 of each year, covering the preceding calendar year. Not later than April 30 of each year, the Secretary of Health and Human Services shall publish on a publicly accessible website each report submitted under this subsection in that year and the name of each covered system from which no report was received by March 31 of that year. The duties to submit and to publish the report, and the duty of the Secretary to publish, are ministerial. Any person may request the published report from a covered system, and the duty to publish is owed to each person who requests it; a person whose request is refused, or is not answered within 30 days, may bring a civil action in the district court of the United States for the district in which the person resides or, where the covered system is not an agency of the United States, for a district in which the covered system operates, to compel publication, an action in the nature of the traditional writ compelling the performance of a ministerial public duty. Any person may bring the same action without a prior request; if the preceding clause is held invalid, the action of a person whose request was refused or not answered stands. Any person may request the publication the Secretary's duty under this subsection requires, and that duty is owed to each person who requests it and is enforceable by the same action, brought in the district court of the United States for the district in which the person resides or for the District of Columbia, with or without a prior request. An action under this subsection concerning the report of a covered system that is not an agency of the United States may be brought against the covered system, against each officer or employee responsible for its compliance with this subsection in that person's official capacity, or against both; and the relief this subsection states is available against each such officer or employee whether or not it is available against the covered system. Relief on a claim to compel publication under this subsection is an order directing the report's submission and publication; a declaratory judgment; any further order necessary to carry out those orders, other than an order that restrains or delays a publication or that requires an inquiry or a record that this Act or an amendment made by this Act forbids; and costs. Where the court finds that the report has not been submitted or published as this subsection requires, it shall order its submission and publication not later than the earliest date on which it finds they can be performed, and neither the defendant's other priorities, nor the resources available to the defendant, nor any other equitable consideration is a ground for withholding that order. A covered system's failure to keep a count this subsection requires is not a ground for finding that the report cannot be submitted or published; the order directing submission and publication directs that the report state each count the covered system can establish and, for each count it did not keep, that it was not kept. An order directing publication directs publication, and not a disclosure to the plaintiff alone; the duty to publish is owed to the plaintiff whole, and its performance in full is the complete relief to which the plaintiff is entitled. For purposes of section 2412 of title 28, United States Code, no provision of this section shall be treated as otherwise specifically providing for, or as expressly prohibiting, an award of costs, fees, or other expenses under that section; and the relief this subsection states supports no inference as to the relief on a claim to enforce a duty that any other provision of this Act makes enforceable. The one-way rule of this subsection is unaffected by the report, and no individual-level record is created by it; a count that records nothing about any individual or encounter other than the State, class of device, or cause under which this subsection requires it to be reported is not an individual-level record. A record made in violation of this subsection's rule against individual-level records, or of the first sentence of section 5-2(b), is subject to subparagraphs (B) and (C) of section 40901(m)(5) of title 34, United States Code, as if it were information described in that paragraph, and shall be destroyed. The Secretary of Health and Human Services shall submit any collection of information necessary to carry out this subsection under section 3507 of title 44, United States Code, not later than 180 days after enactment. The reporting requirement of this subsection takes effect on the earlier of the date a control number is assigned or one year after enactment; on that date section 3512 of that title does not bar an action to compel a report or its publication under this subsection; and the first report is due on the first March 31 after that date, covering the part of the preceding calendar year after that date.\n\n**(c-1) Shortfall statement.** The Secretary of Health and Human Services shall aggregate the reports under subsection (c) and shall publish, not later than June 30 of each year, for each class of device, nationally and for each State, the share of all requests for a device of that class reported under that subsection for the preceding calendar year that were reported as unfilled for want of stock or want of an eligible device. Where, in any calendar year, the requests for a device of either class reported under subsection (c) as unfilled for want of stock or want of an eligible device exceed the share set in the SCHEDULE of all requests for a device of that class reported under that subsection, nationally or in any State, the Secretary shall state that the condition of this subsection is met, in a report transmitted to the Congress not later than June 30 of the following year, together with the sum of the amount appropriated under section 5-2(c) for the fiscal year ending in that year and, for each class of device, the product of the number of requests for a device of that class so reported nationally for that year and the average unit cost of devices of that class furnished under section 5-2 in that year or, where none was furnished in that year, the unit cost at which the Secretary most recently acquired a device of that class; where the Secretary has never acquired a device of a class, the statement gives the number of requests for a device of that class so reported and states that no device of that class has been acquired. The statement is made for each year in which the condition of this subsection is met, and the Secretary shall publish it when it is transmitted. The Secretary shall also publish, not later than June 30 of each year, for each class of device, nationally and for each State, the number of devices supplied under subsection (c-2)(2) in the preceding calendar year after the date by which the supply request stated the device was to be held, stating separately the number for which the supply request was received later than subsection (c-2)(2) requires it to be made; that number is not an input to the condition of this subsection. The duties stated in this subsection are ministerial, are owed to each person who requests a publication or statement this subsection requires, and are enforceable by the action, and with the relief, that subsection (c) states for the Secretary's duty to publish. The transmittal required by this subsection is expressly authorized by Congress within the meaning of section 1913 of title 18, United States Code.\n\n**(c-2) Device held for pickup.** Where a device of a class section 5-2(b) permits to be furnished to an individual at the visit or other contact at which the individual requests it is not furnished there \u2014\n  1. the covered system or other channel at which the request was made (the issuing channel) shall, at that visit or contact, give the individual a notice that bears a serial number made up of an identifier of the issuing channel and a number the issuing channel has not used before and that is not derivable from any other number it has issued; states the class of device requested; states the site of the issuing channel at which it will be held, which is the site of the request unless the individual chooses another of its sites or, for a request made other than at a site of the issuing channel, the site the individual chooses or, if the individual chooses none, a site the issuing channel has designated in advance for such requests; states the date by which paragraph (2) requires it to be held, and that it may be collected during the period set in the SCHEDULE running from that date or, if the device is held later, from the date it is held; and bears no name or other information identifying the individual. The notice is given in writing or, at a visit or contact conducted by telecommunication, by electronic means in a form that leaves, in any system of the issuing channel or of any person acting for it, no record linking the notice to the individual or to any address or account of the individual;\n  2. the issuing channel shall hold a device of that class meeting the standard of section 5-1 at that site not later than the end of the period set in the SCHEDULE, running from the request. Where it does not hold such a device in its own stock for the notice, the issuing channel shall request the device from the Secretary of Health and Human Services not later than the first business day after the notice is issued, stating only the class of device, the site, and the date by which this paragraph requires it to be held, and the Secretary shall supply the issuing channel with that device in time for it to be so held;\n  3. the issuing channel shall furnish the device to any person who presents the notice, without inquiry into identity or firearm ownership, at any time before the end of the period set in the SCHEDULE running from the later of the date by which paragraph (2) requires it to be held and the date on which it is held, after which the device returns to the issuing channel's stock under section 5-2. A person who presents the notice before the device is held shall be told that the device is not yet held and that the period for collection runs from the date it is held; and\n  4. the issuing channel's record of the notices it has issued (the serial record) \u2014\n     (A) states, for each notice, only its serial number, the class of device, the site, the date by which paragraph (2) requires the device to be held, whether the device was held by that date and, if it was held later, the date it was held, and whether the notice was redeemed;\n     (B) may be used only to carry out this subsection and to make the counts subsection (c) requires;\n     (C) is not an individual-level record of acceptance or declination for purposes of subsection (c); a record that states anything subparagraph (A) does not list is not a serial record, and the sentence of subsection (c) governing a record made in violation of that subsection applies to it;\n     (D) may not be joined, matched, or compared with any visit, appointment, encounter, claim, or dispensing record, except that an audit may verify compliance with this paragraph by any means that makes no such join, match, or comparison;\n     (E) is not subject to subpoena, discovery, or other compulsory process; may not be disclosed to any person except to carry out this subsection; and is inadmissible, and may not be the basis of testimony, in any proceeding other than one to enforce this section. If the preceding sentence is held invalid as to compulsory process in any proceeding, the record may be produced in that proceeding only to the court, for inspection in camera, and only the part of it the court finds necessary;\n     (F) states each date as to a notice by year only, once the period for collection of that notice under paragraph (3) has ended; and\n     (G) shall be destroyed not later than 30 days after the later of the end of the last period for collection of a notice issued in a calendar year and the submission of the report under subsection (c) covering that year, except that the record of a notice that is the subject of a pending action is held until the action is finally disposed of.\n     The record the Secretary keeps of supply requests under paragraph (2) is subject to subparagraphs (B) through (G) as if it were a serial record.\nNo payment is made to any individual under this subsection. A device held under it is furnished from stock funded under section 5-2(c). Subchapter I of chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code, does not apply to a notice or record this subsection describes, or to any transmission, between an issuing channel and the Secretary, of the class of device, the site, or the dates a notice states. The duty of the Secretary stated in paragraph (2) is ministerial and is owed to the issuing channel and to each holder of a notice whose period for collection under paragraph (3) has not ended; and the duties paragraphs (2) and (3) place on an issuing channel that is the Veterans Health Administration, the Military Health System, or the Indian Health Service are ministerial and are owed to each such holder. A person to whom such a duty is owed may bring a civil action to enforce it in the district court of the United States for the district in which the site the notice states is located, or for the District of Columbia. The district courts have jurisdiction of the action, the United States consents to it, and the relief it seeks is specific relief and not money damages. Relief on a claim to enforce such a duty is an order directing the duty's performance; a declaratory judgment; any further order necessary to carry out those orders; and costs. Where the court finds that such a duty has not been performed as this subsection requires, it shall order its performance not later than the earliest date on which it finds the duty can be performed, and neither the defendant's other priorities, nor the resources available to the defendant other than the amounts appropriated by section 5-2(c), nor any other equitable consideration is a ground for withholding that order. An action by the holder of a notice may be brought in the serial number of the notice, and the holder's identity is not a condition of any relief; if this sentence is held invalid in that respect, the holder may proceed under a pseudonym, and the court shall keep the holder's identity under seal. For purposes of section 2412 of title 28, United States Code, no provision of this section shall be treated as otherwise specifically providing for, or as expressly prohibiting, an award of costs, fees, or other expenses under that section; and the relief this subsection states supports no inference as to the relief on a claim to enforce a duty that any other provision of this Act makes enforceable.\n\n**(d) Conforming amendment.** Section 511(b) of title 38, United States Code, is amended by striking \"and\" at the end of paragraph (3), by striking the period at the end of paragraph (4) and inserting \"; and\", and by adding at the end the following new paragraph: \"(5) matters arising under section 4-2 of the Community Violence Reduction Act.\" As amended, subsection (b) will read:\n\n> (b) The second sentence of subsection (a) does not apply to\u2014 (1) matters subject to section 502 of this title; (2) matters covered by sections 1975 and 1984 of this title; (3) matters arising under chapter 37 of this title; ~~and~~ (4) matters covered by chapter 72 of this title~~.~~**; and (5) matters arising under section 4-2 of the Community Violence Reduction Act.**\n\nThe omission of any broader exception to section 511(a) of title 38, United States Code, is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Lethal-means counseling training.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 SUICIDE PREVENTION AND MEANS SAFETY","body":"The Secretary of Health and Human Services shall support training of clinicians in lethal-means counseling. There is appropriated for that purpose, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, for each fiscal year, the annual amount set in the SCHEDULE, to remain available until expended, and available without further appropriation. A curriculum qualifies if it meets the content standard stated for training programs in section 2-1(c), applied to means-safety counseling; the Secretary's published list of qualifying curricula is declaratory, and a curriculum meeting the standard qualifies whether or not listed."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Crisis follow-up.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 SUICIDE PREVENTION AND MEANS SAFETY","body":"Amounts set in the SCHEDULE are appropriated for each fiscal year, on the terms stated in section 3-1(b), for follow-up contact and means-safety support, through the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline (operating under section 520E-3 of the Public Health Service Act (42 U.S.C. 290bb-36c)), for individuals who contacted the Lifeline and consented to follow-up."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Device standard.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 SECURE STORAGE","body":"A device meets the standard of this section if, when properly engaged, it prevents a child under the age set in the SCHEDULE, using tools of the classes enumerated in the SCHEDULE, from gaining access to the firearm it secures or, for a device described in subparagraph (A) or (B) of section 921(a)(34) of title 18, United States Code, from firing that firearm and, where the device is detachable, from removing it; and if it retains function after the number of engagement cycles set in the SCHEDULE. The Attorney General shall publish a list of device models meeting the standard not later than one year after enactment, and shall revise it not less often than annually; the duties stated in this sentence are ministerial. The list is declaratory, and a device meeting the standard qualifies whether or not it appears on the list.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 SECURE STORAGE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Device distribution.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 SECURE STORAGE","body":"**(a) Program.** The Secretary of Health and Human Services shall furnish secure storage devices, at no charge to the recipient, through the covered systems described in section 4-2(a), practices furnishing primary care, including pediatric, family-medicine, internal-medicine, geriatric, and obstetric and gynecologic practices, electing to participate, and entities holding awards under title I, other than law enforcement agencies, electing to participate. A practice or entity elects to participate by a written election delivered to the Secretary. A practice's statement in its election that it furnishes primary care is conclusive for purposes of this subsection. On delivery, the practice or entity is an issuing channel by operation of this subsection, is bound by the duties section 4-2(c-2) places on an issuing channel as conditions of its receipt of devices under this section, and is entitled to receive devices under this section on request. Not later than 30 days after an election is delivered, the Secretary shall furnish the practice or entity the instrument by which devices are furnished; the absence of the instrument affects no right or duty under this section. The instrument states the duties section 4-2(c-2) places on an issuing channel; that title to each device remains in the United States until the device is furnished to a recipient; that a device held for pickup and not collected returns to the channel's stock as section 4-2(c-2)(3) provides; and the conditions generally applicable to Federal assistance, other than those subsection (a-2) displaces; and it states no other term or condition. The duties of the Secretary to furnish the instrument and to furnish devices on request to a practice or entity that has elected to participate are ministerial and are owed to the practice or entity, which may enforce them by the action, and with the relief, that section 4-2(c-2) states for the duty of the Secretary stated there, brought in the district court of the United States for a district in which the practice or entity operates or for the District of Columbia.\n\n**(a-1) Acquisition.** The Secretary shall acquire for furnishing under this section and section 4-2 only models of device on the list section 5-1 requires, or models that a testing laboratory has found to meet the standard of section 5-1 in a report the Secretary publishes before acquiring them. The laboratory must be independent of the model's manufacturer and of each seller of the model, as section 2-1(c) states independence for a body and a training program: it is under common ownership or control with none of them, and no compensation it receives from or through any of them depends on its finding.\n\n**(a-2) Award reporting and exclusion.** Notwithstanding the Federal Funding Accountability and Transparency Act of 2006 (Public Law 109-282; 31 U.S.C. 6101 note), section 2(b)(1)(E) of that Act and part 25 of title 2, Code of Federal Regulations (or successor regulations), shall not apply with respect to devices furnished under this section or section 4-2, and no registration or unique entity identifier may be required of a practice or entity as a condition of receiving them; every other requirement of that Act continues to apply. Section 2455 of the Federal Acquisition Streamlining Act of 1994 (31 U.S.C. 6101 note) continues to apply, and the Secretary shall make each check of exclusions it requires for a practice or entity by the name and address of the practice or entity.\n\n**(b) One way; classes of device.** A device shall be furnished on request, without inquiry into firearm ownership and without any individual-level record of the request or the recipient other than the serial record section 4-2(c-2)(4) describes, and no instrument under subsection (a) may require any other record. The devices furnished under this section and section 4-2 are \u2014\n  1. cable locks, furnished on request through any channel described in subsection (a); and\n  2. lock boxes, furnished only at a clinical visit, to the individual receiving health care or to any adult accompanying that individual, without inquiry into the adult's relationship to that individual, or on presentation of a notice under section 4-2(c-2) issued at a clinical visit, and not more than one at any visit.\nNo other device may be furnished under this section or section 4-2.\n\n**(b-1) Definitions.** For purposes of this section and section 4-2 \u2014\n  1. \"cable lock\" means a secure storage device described in section 921(a)(34)(A) of title 18, United States Code, that secures a firearm by a cable or other flexible member passed through its action, magazine well, or barrel;\n  2. \"lock box\" means a portable secure storage device described in section 921(a)(34)(C) of that title whose internal volume does not exceed the volume set in the SCHEDULE; and\n  3. \"clinical visit\" means a visit at which an individual receives health care from a covered system described in section 4-2(a) or from a practice participating under subsection (a), including a visit conducted by telecommunication.\n\n**(c) Appropriation.** There is appropriated for the program, for each fiscal year, the annual amount set in the SCHEDULE, on the terms stated in section 3-1(b).\n\n**(d) Not remuneration.** A device or service furnished under section 4-2 or this section shall not be treated as remuneration for purposes of section 1128A(a)(5) or 1128B(b) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1320a-7a(a)(5), 1320a-7b(b)) or of section 220 of title 18, United States Code."},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Extension of the existing dealer duty.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 SECURE STORAGE","body":"**Amendment.** Section 922(z) of title 18, United States Code, is amended by striking \"handgun\" each place it appears and inserting \"firearm\". As amended, paragraph (1) will read:\n\n> (1) In general.\u2014 Except as provided under paragraph (2), it shall be unlawful for any licensed importer, licensed manufacturer, or licensed dealer to sell, deliver, or transfer any ~~handgun~~ **firearm** to any person other than any person licensed under this chapter, unless the transferee is provided with a secure gun storage or safety device (as defined in section 921(a)(34)) for that ~~handgun~~ **firearm**.\n\nThe exceptions stated in section 922(z)(2), including the exceptions for governmental and law-enforcement transfers and for temporary unavailability, continue to apply as amended."},{"anchor":"sec-9-1","heading":"\u00a7 9-1. Rules of construction.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Nothing in this Act prohibits, conditions, delays, or burdens the acquisition or possession of any firearm by any person, and nothing in this Act authorizes the seizure, confiscation, or compelled surrender of any firearm.\n\n**(b)** Nothing in this Act conditions any Federal benefit, service, or payment on whether a person owns a firearm, on the disclosure of firearm ownership, or on acceptance of any offer this Act requires to be made.\n\n**(c)** The prohibition stated in section 3-5(c) applies to every activity under this Act, regardless of the authority invoked.\n\n**(d)** Nothing in this Act dictates the content of Tribal law; where this Act funds or recognizes a Tribal government or Tribal health program, it imposes duties only as to functions voluntarily assumed.\n\n**(e)** The value of any device, service, counseling, or follow-up contact furnished to an individual under this Act is excluded from the gross income of the recipient for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and is disregarded in determining eligibility for, or the amount of benefit under, any Federal or federally assisted program.\n\n**(f) A floor, and not a ceiling.** No provision of this Act, and no amendment made by this Act, is to be construed as indicating an intent on the part of Congress to occupy the field in which it operates to the exclusion of the law of any State, political subdivision, or Indian Tribe on the same subject matter, unless there is a direct and positive conflict between the provision and that law so that the two cannot be reconciled or consistently stand together. A State, a political subdivision, or an Indian Tribe may impose any requirement that is more protective than, or in addition to, a requirement of this Act, and may operate its own program of the kind this Act funds on any terms not inconsistent with it; every requirement of this Act is a minimum and not a maximum. Section 927 of title 18, United States Code, states this rule for chapter 44 of that title. This subsection does not qualify section 3-5(c), which binds every officer, employee, and agency of the United States, or the exclusive-use and destruction rules of the subsection added by section 4-1, which bind every person into whose hands the information comes. Each of those states a limit and not a minimum, and nothing in this subsection permits a State, a political subdivision, or an Indian Tribe to obtain, retain, or build upon information those provisions close.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-9-2","heading":"\u00a7 9-2. Severability.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or any application of a provision, is held invalid, every other provision and application stands. Each numbered section of this Act operates independently; no court shall treat the provisions of this Act, or of this Act together with any other Act, as an integrated scheme whose parts stand or fall together; and the invalidity of any provision shall not be the basis for any inference about any other."},{"anchor":"sec-9-3","heading":"\u00a7 9-3. Effective dates.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Sections 2-1 and 2-2 apply to services furnished on or after the first day of the first calendar quarter beginning more than 180 days after enactment. The amendment made by section 4-1 takes effect one year after enactment. Section 5-3 applies to transfers more than 180 days after enactment. Every other provision takes effect on enactment."},{"anchor":"sec-9-4","heading":"\u00a7 9-4. Resources and continuity of operations.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** The appropriations made by sections 1-1(b), 3-1(b), 3-2, 4-3, 4-4, and 5-2(c) are permanent, are not subject to apportionment delay or programmatic withholding, and shall be obligated in the fiscal year for which they are made.\n\n**(b)** Continuity of functions and personnel during a lapse in appropriations.\n1. *Functions that continue.* The following continue during any lapse in appropriations: the functions of the national instant criminal background check system under the subsection added by section 4-1; every function title I requires; the publication duties of sections 1-4(b) and 3-4, and the preservation duty of section 3-4(a); the query and audit functions of section 3-5; the offers section 4-2(b) requires; the functions of section 4-2(c), (c-1), and (c-2); the furnishing of devices under section 5-2; the follow-up contact under section 4-4; and the award, obligation, and disbursement of amounts, and the processing of payments, under any grant, contract, compact, cooperative agreement, or other instrument financing any of those functions.\n2. *Pay follows the source of funds.* An officer or employee of the United States who performs a function paragraph (1) names, and whose compensation is payable from an appropriation or other source of funds that has not lapsed, including the appropriations made by sections 1-1(b), 3-1(b), 3-2, 4-3, 4-4, and 5-2(c), continues in duty status and is paid from that source on the ordinary schedule, and section 1341(c)(2) of title 31, United States Code, does not delay that pay.\n3. *Other federal performers.* As to an officer or employee of the United States who performs a function paragraph (1) names and whose compensation is not so payable, obligations for that officer's or employee's pay during a lapse are authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of title 31, United States Code, and the officer's or employee's personal services are authorized by law for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code; the officer or employee is paid as section 1341(c)(2) of that title provides, and is treated as an excepted employee for purposes of section 1341(c) of that title whether or not any definition of the Office of Personnel Management reaches the officer or employee.\n4. *Status by operation of law.* The status paragraphs (2) and (3) state arises by operation of this subsection at the start of any lapse in appropriations, without designation or other act by any officer, and does not depend on any definition, determination, guidance, or plan of the Office of Personnel Management, the Office of Management and Budget, or any other officer, or on whether any plan for operations during a lapse identifies it.\n5. *Staffing.* During a lapse, each function paragraph (1) names that is performed by officers and employees of the United States shall be performed by officers and employees sufficient to perform it at not less than the level at which it was performed in the last full calendar month before the lapse began.\n6. *No separation or reassignment.* During a lapse in appropriations, and for 90 days after it ends, no officer or employee of the United States performing a function paragraph (1) names may be furloughed, separated by reduction in force, placed on administrative leave, or detailed or reassigned away from that function, and no action to implement or maintain an orderly shutdown of Government operations reaches that function. Nothing in this paragraph limits an officer's or employee's use of leave under section 1341(c)(3) of title 31, United States Code.\n7. *Severability within this subsection.* If paragraph (6) is held invalid as applied to separation by reduction in force, administrative leave, detail, or reassignment, the bar on furlough stands, and paragraphs (2) through (5) stand in every case.\n8. *Performers who are not federal.* Where a function paragraph (1) names is performed under a grant, contract, compact, cooperative agreement, or award by a person who is not an officer or employee of the United States, amounts obligated or payable under that instrument remain available during a lapse in appropriations, and no officer or employee of the United States may suspend, terminate, delay, or withhold performance of or payment under that instrument by reason of the lapse.\n9. *States and Tribes.* Nothing in this subsection directs a State, a political subdivision of a State, or an Indian Tribe as to its officers or employees. This paragraph is limited to this subsection and does not qualify any duty section 4-2 places on a covered system.\n10. *Publication.* Not later than 30 days before each fiscal year, the head of each department or agency whose officers or employees perform a function paragraph (1) names shall publish the positions having the status paragraphs (2) and (3) state. The duty to publish is ministerial. Any person may request the publication, and the duty is owed to each person who requests it; a person whose request is refused, or is not answered within 30 days, may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States to compel publication, an action in the nature of the traditional writ compelling the performance of a ministerial public duty. Any person may bring the same action without a prior request; if the preceding clause is held invalid, the action of a person whose request was refused or not answered stands. A failure to publish does not affect any person's status under this subsection.\n\n**(c)** For any function of this Act not funded by a permanent appropriation, or funded by a permanent appropriation as to which the condition of section 4-2(c-1) is met on the reports under section 4-2(c) for the most recent calendar year for which those reports were due before the budget is submitted, whether or not the statement section 4-2(c-1) requires has been made, the President's budget shall state for each fiscal year the amount necessary to perform the function fully, identified as such."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE.","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 1-1(b) Fund annual amount | $5,000,000,000, indexed as the note to this SCHEDULE provides | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 1-1(d)(2) evaluation set-aside; also read by \u00a7 1-3(a-3) | 3 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 1-1(d)(3) administration cap; also read by \u00a7 1-3(a-3) | 4 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 1-3(c) new-program reserve; also read by \u00a7 1-3(a-1) and (a-3)(2) | 15 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 3-1(b) research annual amount | $1,200,000,000, indexed as the note to this SCHEDULE provides | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 3-2 NVDRS annual amount | $60,000,000, indexed as the note to this SCHEDULE provides | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 3-4(a) number of most-traced dealers; also read by Gun Trafficking Act \u00a7 6-5(a) | 200 dealers | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 3-4(b) minimum cell size | 10 records | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 4-3 training annual amount | $50,000,000 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 4-4 crisis follow-up annual amount | $150,000,000, indexed as the note to this SCHEDULE provides | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 5-1 child age | 12 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 5-1 tool classes | household hand tools | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 5-1 engagement cycles | 10,000 cycles | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 5-2(c) distribution annual amount | $400,000,000, indexed as the note to this SCHEDULE provides | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 4-2(c-1) share of reported requests unfilled for want of stock or of an eligible device at which a statement is made | [___ percent] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 4-2(c-2)(2) period, running from the request, by which a requested device not furnished at the visit or other contact is held for pickup | [___ days] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-18 | \u00a7 3-4(a) period after first retail sale within which a recovered firearm counts for the time-to-crime ranking | [___ years] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-19 | \u00a7 1-3(c) number of years over which each rate of firearm homicide is averaged | [___ years] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-20 | \u00a7 1-4(a-1) reduction in the primary outcome measure each design must have the power to detect | [___ percent] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-21 | \u00a7 1-4(a) period within which each model a funded program's fidelity plan identifies is evaluated | [___ years] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-22 | \u00a7 5-2(b-1)(2) maximum internal volume of a lock box | [___ cubic inches] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-23 | \u00a7 4-2(c-2)(3) period, running from the later of the \u00a7 4-2(c-2)(2) date and the date the device is held, during which a device held for pickup may be collected before it returns to stock; also stated in the \u00a7 4-2(c-2)(1) notice | [___ days] | *(Reserved.)* |\n\n**Note \u2014 indexed amounts.** Each amount the SCHEDULE states for S-1, S-6, S-7, S-11, and S-15 is the amount for the fiscal year in which this Act is enacted. For each later fiscal year, the amount is the amount for the preceding fiscal year increased by that fiscal year's increase, the result rounded to the nearest $1,000. A fiscal year's increase is the Middle-Class Growth Rate defined in section 1-1 of the People's Prosperity Act, as most recently certified under section 1-3(a) of that Act before the fiscal year begins, or, where no value has been so certified within the 15 months before the fiscal year begins, including while that Act is not in effect, the percentage change in the Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics from the month 12 months before the most recent month for which it is published before the fiscal year begins to that most recent month, using each month's value as first published; an increase less than zero counts as zero. Each amount changes by operation of this note, without action by any person, and the Secretary of the Treasury shall publish each amount for a fiscal year before the fiscal year begins; the duty to publish is ministerial, and a failure to publish does not affect the amount."}],"asOf":"2026-11-15"}}}};
